changes.old_string:
- `/cabinet` and standalone app-entry messages may generate a plain URL button to `/app/browser-login/<signed-token>/`;
- this URL is for Safari/Chrome/Home Screen sessions and is shown before the Telegram Web App action on those messages, while the persistent Telegram chat menu remains `MenuButtonWebApp`;
- known embedded mobile browsers must keep the handoff non-consuming and must not present the final login action until the same handoff is opened in Safari/Chrome;
- the handoff's final action uses `/app/api/browser-login/<signed-token>/`, because the existing Mini App Service Worker bypasses `/app/api/*` and therefore cannot interfere with Safari's session-setting redirect. `/app/login/<signed-token>/` remains a legacy-compatible consumer only;
- the signed token is the identity proof, not a trusted `tg_user_id` query parameter.
- expired or already-used token pages and the unauthenticated standalone `/app/` shell must offer `https://t.me/vydnocapital_bot?start=app_login` so the bot can generate a fresh signed URL.

changes.new_string:
- `/cabinet` and standalone app-entry messages use a public HTTPS `/app/login/` URL, not a user-bound signed token; the intended user must confirm their own identity through Telegram OIDC.
- the browser entry precedes the Telegram Web App action on these messages, while the persistent Telegram chat menu remains `MenuButtonWebApp`;
- known embedded mobile browsers retain non-consuming external-browser handoff before OIDC state creation;
- URL identity parameters never grant authentication or operator permissions; state, PKCE, signature/claims and replay checks remain required.

Legacy signed-link compatibility only:

- previously issued `/app/browser-login/<signed-token>/` pages and `/app/api/browser-login/<signed-token>/` consumers remain compatible, with one-time signed identity proof and external-browser handoff;
- expired/used legacy tokens and unauthenticated shells offer `https://t.me/vydnocapital_bot?start=app_login` for the current confirmation entry, not a new ordinary signed-link issuance path.
